---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Row from '../components/Row.astro';
import Note from '../components/Note.astro';
import NoteRef from '../components/NoteRef.astro';
import PostList from '../components/PostList.astro';
import AgentStep from '../components/figures/AgentStep.astro';
import EnergyAxis from '../components/figures/EnergyAxis.astro';
import LifeGraph from '../components/figures/LifeGraph.astro';
import NetGlyph from '../components/figures/NetGlyph.astro';
import RiverGauge from '../components/figures/RiverGauge.astro';
import Portrait from '../assets/profile.jpg';
import {
  EMAIL,
  GITHUB_URL,
  LINKEDIN_URL,
  SITE_DESCRIPTION,
  SITE_TITLE,
} from '../consts';
import { sitePath } from '../lib/paths';

const RIVER_URL = 'https://river.edjex.dev';
const TDMPC2_URL = 'https://github.com/Jex-y/tdmpc2-jax';
const thesis = sitePath('/blog/final-year-project/');
const cisco = sitePath('/blog/cisco-internship/');
---

<BaseLayout title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION}>
  <main class="page">
    <Hero />

    <Row>
      <p>
        I'm a software engineer at Solve Intelligence, where I build AI tools
        for patent attorneys.<NoteRef n={1} />
      </p>
      <p>
        I studied computer science at Durham and finished top of my year.<NoteRef
          n={2}
        /> I also learned to row there, and spent a year running Trevelyan College
        Boat Club. Needing to know whether the river was safe to go out on turned
        into a forecast of the River Wear.<NoteRef n={3} />
      </p>
      <p>
        For my master's thesis I searched five hundred neural networks for one
        that could find water fleas on almost no power. The best of them had
        three layers.<NoteRef n={4} />
      </p>

      <Fragment slot="left">
        <figure class="portrait">
          <Image
            src={Portrait}
            alt="Ed Jex at his graduation in Durham"
            width={360}
            height={450}
            fit="cover"
          />
          <figcaption>Durham, 2025.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <Note n={1}>
          Mostly agentic claim charting, and the systems that keep thousands of
          agents running at once. More under Work.
          <svg
            slot="figure"
            viewBox="0 0 72 30"
            width="72"
            height="30"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <g stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.9">
              <line x1="36" y1="15" x2="8" y2="5"></line>
              <line x1="36" y1="15" x2="8" y2="25"></line>
              <line x1="36" y1="15" x2="64" y2="15"></line>
            </g>
            <circle cx="36" cy="15" r="3.6" fill="#000000"></circle>
            <circle cx="8" cy="5" r="2.6" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#000000"
            ></circle>
            <circle cx="8" cy="25" r="2.6" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#000000"
            ></circle>
            <circle cx="64" cy="15" r="2.6" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#000000"
            ></circle>
          </svg>
        </Note>
        <Note n={3}>
          The clubs check it before they boat: <a href={RIVER_URL}
            >river.edjex.dev</a
          >
        </Note>
      </Fragment>

      <Fragment slot="right">
        <Note n={2}>
          MEng, first class, with the prizes for the top project and the top
          overall grade.
        </Note>
        <Note n={4}>
          2.2 µJ a frame on a Raspberry Pi with a Hailo-8L. More under Research.
          <NetGlyph
            slot="figure"
            width={72}
            height={36}
            radius={2.4}
            layers={[
              { x: 8, ys: [6, 14, 22, 30] },
              { x: 36, ys: [6, 14, 22, 30] },
              { x: 64, ys: [12, 24] },
            ]}
          />
        </Note>
        <LifeGraph />
      </Fragment>
    </Row>

    <Row><h2 id="work">Work</h2></Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        At Solve I've mostly worked on agents. I took agentic claim charting
        from a first prototype to something attorneys use,<NoteRef n={5} /> and built
        a good part of the system underneath it.
      </p>
      <Note slot="left" n={5}>
        A claim chart maps each element of a patent claim to evidence in prior
        art or a product. In production since [MONTH YEAR].
      </Note>
    </Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        Three problems took most of the time. Making each step of the tool loop
        fast, by running independent calls at once and cutting the dead time
        between model turns.<NoteRef n={6} /> Streaming everything an agent does
        to the browser as it happens, so nobody sits watching a spinner. And hosting:
        scaling our agent runtime to thousands of agent clusters working at once,
        over long trajectories.<NoteRef n={7} />
      </p>
      <Note slot="left" n={7}>
        A cluster is a group of agents sharing one task. Trajectories run to [N]
        steps.
      </Note>
      <Fragment slot="right">
        <Note n={6}>About [X]× faster per step.</Note>
        <AgentStep />
      </Fragment>
    </Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        Before Solve I spent a summer at Cisco, working on the Webex web client.<NoteRef
          n={8}
        />
      </p>
      <Note slot="right" n={8}>
        Twelve weeks, summer 2022. <a href={cisco}>I wrote about it</a>.
      </Note>
    </Row>

    <Row><h2 id="research">Research</h2></Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        My thesis asked how little energy a useful vision model can spend. The
        task was watching water fleas, which spin when the water around them is
        polluted, on a device powered by a microbial fuel cell. Treating
        detection as finding points rather than drawing boxes, then searching
        five hundred architectures and measuring each one on the hardware, gave
        a three-layer network that uses 2.2 µJ a frame: 680 times less than
        YOLO11-nano.<NoteRef n={9} />
      </p>
      <Note slot="left" n={9}>
        Supervised by Dr Farshad Arvin. <a href={thesis}>The full write-up</a>.
      </Note>
      <EnergyAxis slot="right">
        Energy per frame in µJ, measured on the device. Log scale.
      </EnergyAxis>
    </Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        On the side I rewrote TD-MPC2, a model-based reinforcement learning
        method, in JAX. Compiling the planning and update steps makes it train
        five to ten times faster than the original.<NoteRef n={10} /> For two years
        I also ran undergraduate labs, in Computational Thinking and in Networks
        and Systems.
      </p>
      <Note slot="right" n={10}>
        JAX, Flax and MuJoCo. <a href={TDMPC2_URL}
          >github.com/Jex-y/tdmpc2-jax</a
        >
      </Note>
    </Row>

    <Row><h2 id="river">On the river</h2></Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        I learned to row at Durham, and in 2023 I became president of Trevelyan
        College Boat Club: a year of training plans, races, budgets and boats.
        Whether we could go out at all depended on the Wear, so in 2024 I built
        a deep-learning forecast of the river level and a dashboard the clubs
        check before they boat.<NoteRef n={11} />
      </p>
      <Note slot="left" n={11}>
        PyTorch for the model, Rust and ONNX to serve it. <a href={RIVER_URL}
          >river.edjex.dev</a
        >
      </Note>
      <RiverGauge slot="right" />
    </Row>

    <Row><h2 id="writing">Writing</h2></Row>

    <PostList />

    <Row><h2 id="cv">Elsewhere</h2></Row>

    <Row>
      <p class="elsewhere">
        Write to <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>. Code is on <a
          href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a
        >, the rest is on <a href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a>, and there's a <a
          href={sitePath('/cv.pdf')}>CV</a
        > if you want one.
      </p>
    </Row>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .portrait {
    gap: 6px;
  }

  .portrait img {
    display: block;
    width: 160px;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .elsewhere a {
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1180px) {
    .portrait img {
      width: 180px;
    }
  }
</style>
